<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Imamate of Nasir al-Din</h1><p class="st-meta">1673 CE – 1677 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived Islamic reform movement led by the Berber scholar Nasir al-Din, which overthrew the kings of the lower Senegal valley in the 1670s.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-al-din-mauritanian-cleric">Nasir al-Din</a> was a cleric of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zawaya">Zawaya</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> scholarly tribes of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, who resented the dominance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hassan">Hassani Arab</a> warriors and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> with the French at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-louis-senegal">Saint-Louis</a>. Around 1673 he proclaimed a holy war, known as the Tubenan movement, calling for rule by Islamic law.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>His followers overthrew or converted the rulers of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/waalo">Waalo</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cayor">Kayor</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-jolof">Kingdom of Jolof</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/imamate-of-futa-toro">Futa Toro</a>, replacing them with clerics loyal to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>. The movement drew on peasant grievances against kings who sold their subjects into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-al-din-mauritanian-cleric">Nasir al-Din</a> was killed in battle against Hassani warriors in 1674.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under his successors the movement weakened. The old ruling families, supported by the French, regained their thrones by 1677, and the Hassani defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zawaya">Zawaya</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/char-bouba-war">Char Bouba war</a>. The idea of a clerical state inspired later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> revolutions in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/imamate-of-futa-jallon">Futa Jallon</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/imamate-of-futa-toro">Futa Toro</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imamate-of-nasir-al-din.jpg" alt="Imamate of Nasir al-Din" width="320" height="432"><figcaption>Image: Anoniem / Anonymous (land surveyor / mapmaker), Anoniem / Anonymous (publisher) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AMH-8160-KB_Map_of_the_regions_between_the_Senegal_and_Gambra_rivers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1673 CE – 1677 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nasir al-Din</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (Maliki)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Zenaga, Arabic, Wolof</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Mauritania, Senegal</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sharr Bubba, Tubenan movement</dd></dl></aside></div>

